  'what-home-listing-doesnt-tell-you',
  'What a Home Listing Doesn''t Tell You (And Where to Find It)',
  'Every listing has gaps — things the seller isn''t required to disclose, things the photos hide, and things no public database tracks. Here''s what to look for before you make an offer.',
  '/__l5e/assets-v1/81d48249-c532-4918-bed7-9076315862c2/article07-hero.jpg',
$body$
<p>A listing page is built to sell. That''s not a cynical observation — it''s just what it is. The agent''s job is to present the property in its best possible light, and within the bounds of disclosure law, they''re good at it.</p>
<p>What that means in practice is that a listing gives you a complete picture of what the seller wants you to know, and a partial picture of everything else. The gaps aren''t usually dishonest. They''re structural — information that no listing is required to contain, information the seller genuinely doesn''t have, and information that would survive a fact-check but still mislead without context.</p>
<p>Knowing what''s missing is as important as knowing how to read what''s there.</p>

<h2>Quick Answer</h2>
<p>A listing doesn''t tell you the property''s full maintenance history, what prior inspections found, what the neighbors are like, what the actual tax bill will be at your purchase price, what a seller''s disclosure form will reveal (you don''t see that until after your offer is accepted), or what the carrying costs look like beyond the mortgage estimate. Most of this information exists — it just requires knowing where to find it.</p>

<h2>The Seller''s Disclosure — You Don''t See It Until After Your Offer</h2>
<p>This is the one that catches buyers off guard most often.</p>
<p>Most disclosure forms are handed over after the seller accepts your offer — not during the search phase. You''ll usually get a few days to review the information and decide whether to move forward, renegotiate the price, or walk away.</p>
<p>The disclosure form covers what the seller is legally required to report: known material defects, prior repairs, water damage history, pest treatments, permit history, known issues with the roof, HVAC, foundation, and electrical. It''s useful information — but it arrives after you''ve already invested time, effort, and emotional energy into a specific property.</p>
<p>One principle stays constant across all states: the seller can only disclose what they actually know. A seller who has never noticed a slow roof leak isn''t lying when they check "no" on the water intrusion question. They''re reporting their knowledge, not the property''s condition. This is why the home inspection exists as a separate layer of due diligence — the disclosure tells you what the seller knows; the inspection tells you what''s actually there.</p>
<p>A study by Cinch Home Services found that 95% of buyers find issues after closing. If those issues were known but hidden, the buyer has grounds for legal action. But if the seller genuinely didn''t know, there''s no recourse — you own the problem.</p>
<p>The practical takeaway: read the disclosure carefully when you receive it, and never treat it as a substitute for a thorough inspection. It''s a data point, not a clean bill of health.</p>

<h2>The Real Tax Bill at Your Purchase Price</h2>
<p>Listings typically display the current annual property tax — what the seller pays based on their assessed value. That number can be significantly different from what you''ll pay as the new owner.</p>
<p>In most states, the property tax basis resets when ownership transfers. If the current owner bought the home in 2014 for $280,000 and you''re buying it for $480,000, your assessed value — and your tax bill — will likely be calculated at or near the new purchase price, not the old one.</p>
<p>In California, Proposition 13 limits annual increases, which means long-held properties often show tax bills far below what a new buyer will owe. In Texas, there''s no income tax but property taxes are among the highest in the country and reset aggressively at sale. In Florida, the homestead exemption reduces assessed value for owner-occupants — and that exemption doesn''t transfer to you automatically.</p>
<p>The listing shows the current owner''s tax bill. Your tax bill is a different calculation. Before you finalize your monthly cost estimate, contact the county assessor''s office or check the assessor''s website and model what taxes will look like at your purchase price. In some markets, the gap between listed and actual taxes runs $2,000 to $5,000 per year.</p>

<figure>
  <img src="/__l5e/assets-v1/769cce94-c965-4a5f-9fc4-19242ba333ae/article07-tax.jpg" alt="Property tax statement next to a calculator on a desk" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>The tax bill shown in the listing belongs to the current owner''s assessed value — not yours.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Carrying Costs Beyond the Mortgage</h2>
<p>A listing shows a price. Sometimes it shows an estimated mortgage payment, usually calculated at an idealized rate for a specific down payment assumption. What it doesn''t show is the total monthly cost of ownership.</p>
<p>The number you should be calculating before any offer:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Mortgage principal + interest</strong> at your actual rate and down payment — not the listing estimate.</li>
  <li><strong>Property taxes</strong> at your purchase price basis, not the seller''s.</li>
  <li><strong>Homeowners insurance</strong> — typically $100 to $300/month for a single-family home, higher in flood, hurricane, or wildfire zones. Rates in some coastal markets have increased significantly in 2025–2026 as insurers have repriced climate risk.</li>
  <li><strong>HOA fees</strong> — if applicable, this is listed. But what isn''t always listed: pending special assessments. An HOA with a depleted reserve fund can vote a special assessment at any time, which becomes your financial obligation as the new owner. Request the HOA financials and meeting minutes before closing, not just the monthly fee.</li>
  <li><strong>Flood insurance</strong> — not disclosed in the listing unless the seller currently carries it. Check FEMA''s flood map for the address. Properties in Special Flood Hazard Areas require flood insurance with a federally backed mortgage, at costs that can run $1,500 to $4,000 or more annually.</li>
  <li><strong>Maintenance and repairs</strong> — not a monthly line item on any listing, but budgeted at 1%–2% of property value annually on a stabilized home. On a $400,000 house, that''s $4,000–$8,000 per year that belongs in your affordability model.</li>
</ul>
<p>The monthly number that determines whether you can afford a home is the sum of all of these — not the mortgage payment alone.</p>

<h2>The Maintenance History You Won''t Find Online</h2>
<p>Every home has a history of repairs, replacements, and deferred maintenance. Most of it isn''t visible anywhere.</p>
<p>The listing might say "new roof — 2022." It won''t say whether the roof was replaced because of storm damage that also affected the underlayment, or whether the gutters and fascia were replaced at the same time or left original. It won''t say whether the HVAC was serviced annually or run until it failed. It won''t say whether the water heater is on its third anode rod or still original.</p>
<p>Some of this comes out in the disclosure form. Some comes out in the inspection. A lot of it only surfaces in conversation with the seller — or doesn''t surface at all.</p>
<p>Questions worth asking before or during a showing:</p>
<ul>
  <li>How old are the major systems — roof, HVAC, water heater, electrical panel?</li>
  <li>Have there been any insurance claims on the property in the last five years?</li>
  <li>Were permits pulled for any renovations?</li>
  <li>When was the last time the sewer lateral was scoped?</li>
</ul>
<p>With the cost of repairs for big-ticket items — roof, HVAC, foundation — at all-time highs, buying a home without understanding its maintenance history is a financial risk that''s difficult to recover from. The questions above don''t always get complete answers, but they''re worth asking. An evasive response is itself useful information.</p>

<h2>What''s Actually Around the Property</h2>
<p>The listing describes the property. It doesn''t describe what''s next to it, across the street, or three blocks away — unless those things are selling points.</p>
<p>A home that backs to a commercial parking lot will have a photo of the backyard taken from the angle that excludes the lot. A house across from a vacant strip mall will show a street-level view that frames away from it. A property adjacent to a cell tower will mention the "open views."</p>
<p>This isn''t always deceptive — sellers and their agents don''t have a legal obligation to list every neighboring condition, and buyers are expected to do their own due diligence on location. But it means the listing is an incomplete picture of the surrounding environment.</p>
<p>What to check:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Google Maps satellite view and Street View of the specific address and surrounding blocks</li>
  <li>Walk or drive the surrounding blocks at different times — evening, morning rush, weekend</li>
  <li>Check the zoning of adjacent vacant lots or commercial properties (city planning websites)</li>
  <li>Look up permit applications for neighboring properties in active development</li>
</ul>
<p><a href="/blog/how-to-evaluate-neighborhood-before-buying">The Neighborhoods Pillar</a> covers this in more depth — the short version is that location research belongs in your process before the showing, not after.</p>

<h2>The Prior Listing History</h2>
<p>A listing that appeared on the MLS last week may have been on the MLS six months ago under a different listing ID. It may have been taken off the market to reset the days-on-market counter. It may have had a different price, different photos, or a different description.</p>
<p>None of this is visible on the current listing page. It requires either MLS access through your agent, or checking the price history tab on listing platforms — which shows price changes but often doesn''t show prior delisted periods.</p>
<p>Homes that don''t go under contract within about two weeks begin accumulating a stigma in buyer perception. Sellers know this, which is why relisting to reset the counter happens. The question you''re trying to answer isn''t just how many days this listing has been active — it''s how long this property has been trying to sell.</p>
<p>Ask your agent to pull the full MLS history for any property you''re seriously considering.</p>

<h2>Browsing Tips</h2>
<p><strong>Run an insurance quote before you make an offer, not after.</strong> In Florida, California, Louisiana, and parts of the Gulf Coast, homeowners insurance has become difficult to obtain and significantly more expensive. Getting a quote before you''re emotionally committed to a property is better than discovering the coverage problem after your offer is accepted.</p>
<p><strong>Check permit history at the county level.</strong> Most county assessor or building department websites allow you to search permit records by address. Unpermitted additions, renovations, or structural work create title and insurance complications — and sometimes force costly remediation after you close. A finished basement with no permit on record is a common example.</p>
<p><strong>Request the seller''s utility bills.</strong> Twelve months of electric, gas, and water bills tell you things the listing doesn''t: whether the home runs efficiently, whether there are unexplained usage spikes that suggest a leak or HVAC failure, and what the actual monthly carrying cost looks like across seasons.</p>

<h2>HomeLens Insight</h2>
<p>The financial picture of a listing — what the payment actually looks like at current rates, what the total monthly cost is, how the price compares to what comparable homes have sold for — takes time to assemble from multiple sources. HomeLens AI pulls the analysis together on any listing you''re browsing, so the data gaps narrow before you decide whether the property is worth pursuing further.</p>
<p><a href="/integrations"><strong>Install the HomeLens AI Chrome Extension →</strong></a></p>

<h2>FAQ</h2>
<h3>When do I receive the seller''s disclosure?</h3>
<p>In most states, the seller''s disclosure form is provided after the seller accepts your offer, though some listing agents provide it earlier during showings. You typically receive a few days to review it and decide whether to proceed, renegotiate, or withdraw. If you want to see it before making an offer, ask your agent to request it. Not all sellers will provide it pre-offer, but some will — and seeing it early can save you time on a property with known issues.</p>
<h3>Is a seller required to disclose all problems with the home?</h3>
<p>Sellers are required to disclose known material defects — issues that would significantly affect the property''s value or the safety of occupants. The key constraint is that the seller can only disclose what they actually know. Unknown problems, including defects that a proper inspection would reveal, don''t create disclosure liability for the seller. This is one reason why skipping the inspection is a risk that the listing price alone can''t compensate for.</p>
<h3>What is a special assessment and why does it matter?</h3>
<p>A special assessment is a one-time charge levied by an HOA when the reserve fund doesn''t cover a major repair — a roof replacement on a shared building, a repaved parking lot, a pool renovation. It becomes the financial obligation of whoever owns the unit at the time the assessment is approved or billed. A pending special assessment can mean thousands of dollars in additional cost that never appears in the listing. Always request the HOA''s most recent financial statements and meeting minutes before closing.</p>
<h3>How do I find out if a home has unpermitted work?</h3>
<p>Most county building departments maintain searchable permit records by address. Search the county assessor or building department website for the property address and review what permits are on file. If a finished basement, an added bathroom, or a converted garage doesn''t have a corresponding permit, that work is unpermitted. The implications range from minor (requiring retroactive permitting) to significant (forced removal or remediation at your expense after you own it).</p>

